// A **positional** enum payload may name an imported type — and the fully-qualified path too. All
// four spellings of "wrap a type in a variant" now work; three of them did not, and there was no
// spelling that worked for a cross-module positional payload at all:
//
//   positional, same file       Leaf(Local)             ok
//   named, cross-module         Named(u: User)          ok
//   positional, cross-module    Imported(User)          E0013 "unknown type `User`"
//   positional, qualified       Qualified(App.Models.User)   syntax error
//
// The cause was the representation: a positional payload parsed through the *parameter* grammar,
// so its type landed in the field's `name` with `ty: None`. Module qualification rewrites `ty`, so
// it never saw the type (while the named form's `ty` was rewritten correctly), and an identifier is
// not a type, so a dotted path could not parse. The payload's type lives in `ty` now, which is also
// what lets a positional payload be any type at all — `List<User>`, `?int`, a tuple.
// expect: stdout "ada"
// expect: stdout "bob"
// expect: stdout "cyd"
// expect: stdout "1"
// expect: exit 0
namespace App.Main;

use App.Models.User;

struct Local { tag: string }

enum Wrap {
    Imported(User);
    Qualified(App.Models.User);
    Named(u: User);
    LocalOne(Local);
    Many(List<User>);
}

fn name_of(w: Wrap): string {
    return match w {
        Wrap.Imported(u) => u.name,
        Wrap.Qualified(u) => u.name,
        Wrap.Named(u) => u.name,
        Wrap.LocalOne(l) => l.tag,
        Wrap.Many(us) => "${us.len()}",
    };
}

echo name_of(Wrap.Imported(User { name: "ada" }));
echo name_of(Wrap.Qualified(User { name: "bob" }));
echo name_of(Wrap.Named(User { name: "cyd" }));
echo name_of(Wrap.Many([User { name: "d" }]));
